1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:11,260
You know the feeling – your ears start to warm up, your tongue goes numb, you start sweating and taking deep breaths.
你懂这种感觉——耳朵开始发烫，舌头发麻，浑身出汗，同时还深呼吸。

2
00:00:11,260 --> 00:00:19,910
You've just eaten something spicy knowing it would be painful, and, yet, you chose to do it anyway.
你明知吃辛辣食物会难受，却还是选择去吃。

3
00:00:19,910 --> 00:00:25,560
Are humans just masochistic, or is there something else going on?
人类只是有受虐倾向吗，还是另有原因？

4
00:00:25,560 --> 00:00:29,910
The answer lies in both science and history.
答案藏在科学与历史之中。

5
00:00:29,910 --> 00:00:32,790
Let's start with the science.
我们先从科学角度说起。

6
00:00:32,790 --> 00:00:41,540
Spicy isn't actually a taste like salty, sweet, sour and bitter – it’s a sensation.
辛辣其实并非咸、甜、酸、苦那样的味觉，而是一种感官感受。

7
00:00:41,540 --> 00:00:50,360
This sensation is triggered by a chemical compound found in chilli peppers called 'capsaicin'.
这种感受由辣椒中一种名为“辣椒素”的化合物触发。

8
00:00:50,360 --> 00:00:59,630
When we eat foods containing capsaicin, our bodies are tricked into thinking the temperature is actually rising.
当我们食用含有辣椒素的食物时，身体会误以为体温在升高。

9
00:00:59,630 --> 00:01:13,010
In trying to temper the burning sensation, our bodies release endorphins which control pain and, at the same time, give a feeling of pleasure – like painkillers.
为了缓解灼烧感，身体会释放内啡肽，这种物质既能镇痛，又能带来愉悦感，效果如同止痛药。

10
00:01:13,010 --> 00:01:20,610
This is what is happening chemically, but there is also a conscious side to choosing spicy food.
这是生理层面的化学反应，但选择吃辣还有主观意识层面的原因。

11
00:01:20,610 --> 00:01:41,370
Dr Tamara Rosenbaum, Cognitive Neuroscientist at the National Autonomous University of Mexico, explains in an interview with the BBC that this is because we are rational beings – we know that the burning sensation of chilli does not physically harm us.
墨西哥国立自治大学的认知神经科学家塔玛拉·罗森鲍姆博士在接受BBC采访时解释道，这是因为人类是理性生物——我们清楚辣椒带来的灼烧感并不会对身体造成实质伤害。

12
00:01:41,370 --> 00:01:52,370
Furthermore, we derive pleasure from the other ingredients chilli is generally cooked and eaten with – including fat, sugar and salt.
此外，我们还能从与辣椒一同烹制食用的其他食材中获得愉悦感，比如脂肪、糖和盐。

13
00:01:52,370 --> 00:02:04,530
So – like skydiving – eating chilli is a form of thrill-seeking or 'benign masochism', where we get pleasure from a seemingly negative sensation.
所以，就像跳伞一样，吃辣是一种寻求刺激的行为，或是“良性受虐”，即从看似负面的感官感受中获得快乐。

14
00:02:04,530 --> 00:02:15,090
This masochistic relationship with capsaicin has been a long one, starting in the Andes of South America, where chilli peppers originate.
人类与辣椒素的这种“受虐式”渊源由来已久，起源于南美洲的安第斯山脉，那里也是辣椒的原产地。

15
00:02:15,090 --> 00:02:30,120
Humans were one of the few mammals on Earth that developed a taste for capsaicin so, archaeological evidence suggests, they started cultivating chilli peppers about six thousand years ago.
考古证据表明，人类是地球上少数能接受辣椒素的哺乳动物之一，大约六千年前就开始种植辣椒。

16
00:02:30,120 --> 00:02:46,750
Human intervention changed the chilli pepper to suit human tastes and needs – including the pepper's colour, size and capsaicin content – helping to explain the many different types of chilli peppers now available.
人类的干预改造了辣椒，使其适配人类的口味与需求，比如辣椒的颜色、大小和辣椒素含量，这也解释了如今为何会有种类繁多的辣椒。

17
00:02:46,750 --> 00:02:52,340
Fast-forward to today, and our love affair with the chilli pepper is going strong.
时间快进到如今，我们对辣椒的热爱愈发浓烈。

18
00:02:52,340 --> 00:03:06,020
We eat around 57.3 million tons of peppers globally each year, and chilli is a key ingredient in traditional dishes from Mexico to Korea.
全球每年的辣椒消费量约为5730万吨，从墨西哥到韩国，辣椒都是传统菜肴中的关键食材。

19
00:03:06,020 --> 00:03:08,980
Maybe we are masochistic after all.
或许我们终究是有点受虐倾向吧。
